# OS/7 — packages that must never be installed on an OS/7 desktop.
#
# Written by build/config/hooks-amd64/0035-debrand-desktop.hook.chroot and
# SHIPPED, on purpose: purging at build time lasts only until the first upgrade
# re-satisfies a Recommends of ubuntu-desktop-minimal. This file is what makes
# the removal survive an `apt full-upgrade` and a release upgrade.
#
# Every entry below is a Recommends of ubuntu-desktop-minimal (or, for the
# plymouth theme, of plymouth and ubuntu-standard), and nothing Depends on any
# of them — measured against the shipped image on 2026-08-26. A Recommends that
# cannot be installed is simply skipped by apt, so nothing here can break a
# dependency.
#
# To put one back, delete its stanza. These are defaults, not locks — the same
# rule the dconf database in os7-desktop-theme follows.

# The first-login wizard: language, privacy, "Enable Ubuntu Pro", "Help improve
# Ubuntu". OS/7 asks its questions in os7-setup, before the machine has ever
# booted a desktop, and asks them once.
Package: gnome-initial-setup
Pin: release *
Pin-Priority: -1

# Telemetry to Canonical.
Package: ubuntu-report ubuntu-insights
Pin: release *
Pin-Priority: -1

# Crash reporting to Canonical. A managed fleet reports to whoever manages it.
#
# whoopsie-preferences is NOT in this list and must not be added to it:
# gnome-control-center DEPENDS on it, gnome-shell and ubuntu-desktop-minimal
# depend on gnome-control-center, and purging it takes the entire desktop with
# it. Measured, after it did. docs/BUILD-NOTES.md #87. It is the D-Bus service
# that stores the preference; with whoopsie gone there is nothing for it to
# enable.
Package: whoopsie apport apport-gtk apport-core-dump-handler apport-symptoms
Pin: release *
Pin-Priority: -1

# Ubuntu-branded help content.
Package: ubuntu-docs gnome-user-docs
Pin: release *
Pin-Priority: -1

# The browser of this product is Microsoft Edge (hook 0030). Note that snapd is
# deliberately NOT pinned here: authd-msentraid is a snap.
Package: firefox
Pin: release *
Pin-Priority: -1

# The second terminal. OS/7's package list names gnome-terminal and its dconf
# defaults make it start a login shell, which is what hands the session to
# PowerShell. ptyxis arrives beside it as a Recommends, registers
# x-terminal-emulator at the SAME priority 40, and wins the tie - so "Open in
# Terminal" opened a terminal that lands in bash. One terminal, one answer.
Package: ptyxis
Pin: release *
Pin-Priority: -1

# The Ubuntu wordmark on the text boot splash. Unused while default.plymouth is
# bgrt, and pinned so that it stays unused.
Package: plymouth-theme-ubuntu-text
Pin: release *
Pin-Priority: -1

# Two launchers in the Apps menu that belong to a GNOME developer's desktop and
# not to an administrator's (SESSION-QOL-SURVEY Q2): Sysprof, a CPU profiler,
# and Extensions, which can switch off the four extensions the OS/7 Classic
# desktop is made of. Both are only Recommends - sysprof of
# ubuntu-desktop-minimal, gnome-shell-extension-prefs of the Classic
# extensions themselves - measured with apt-cache rdepends on 1.0.0.247, and a
# purge simulation removed exactly the two. `gnome-extensions` on the command
# line is gnome-shell's and stays.
Package: sysprof gnome-shell-extension-prefs
Pin: release *
Pin-Priority: -1
